
  <sn><md>### Humus and Classification of Humus

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-9
**Definition:**
Humus is the **dark, amorphous, colloidal organic matter** formed by the **decomposition and resynthesis** of plant and animal residues by soil microorganisms. It is highly resistant to further decomposition and is a key component of soil organic matter.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-3
![img](https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcSOIawQM_Phvf14_UvYXc6qavVviF9t4DEI34oyD0n4sg&amp;s=10)
:::
:::

**Characteristics of Humus:**
- Dark brown to black in colour
- **Colloidal** in nature — high surface area
- **High CEC** (200–300 cmol/kg) — retains nutrients
- **High water holding capacity**
- **Buffers soil pH**
- Resistant to microbial decomposition (stable)
- Improves soil structure and aeration

**Composition:**
- Carbon (~55–60%)
- Nitrogen (~3–6%)
- Oxygen, hydrogen, sulfur, phosphorus
- **Humic substances** (fulvic acid, humic acid, humin)
- **Non-humic substances** (proteins, carbohydrates, fats)
