<sn><md>### Classification of Humus {.text-center}

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20and%20Enviromental%20Chemistry/04.Humus/assets/types_of_humus.jpeg)

+++ diagram
```text
TYPES OF HUMUS
│
├── Based on Origin / Formation
│   ├── Mor    → acidic + cold + poorly drained
│   ├── Mull   → neutral/alkaline + well drained
│   └── Moder  → intermediate
│
├── Based on Chemical Nature
│   ├── Fulvic acid → soluble in acid &amp; alkali → most mobile
│   ├── Humic acid  → soluble in alkali → intermediate
│   └── Humin      → insoluble in acid &amp; alkali → most stable
│
└── Based on Decomposition Stage
    ├── Labile humus  → easily decomposed → quick nutrient release
    └── Stable humus  → resistant → slow nutrient release
```
+++

</md></sn><sn><md>#### Based on Origin / Formation

| Type | Description |
|---|---|
| **Raw humus (Mor)** | Formed in acidic, poorly drained, cold conditions; thick O horizon; low microbial activity; e.g., coniferous forest soils |
| **Mull humus** | Formed in neutral to alkaline, well-drained soils; well-mixed with mineral soil; high microbial activity; e.g., deciduous forest soils |
| **Moder humus** | Intermediate between Mor and Mull; moderate decomposition; e.g., grassland soils |

#### Based on Chemical Nature (Humic Substances)

| Type | Description |
|---|---|
| **Fulvic acid** | Soluble in both acid and alkali; light yellow; low molecular weight; high oxygen; most mobile |
| **Humic acid** | Soluble in alkali, insoluble in acid; dark brown; medium molecular weight; intermediate |
| **Humin** | Insoluble in both acid and alkali; black; high molecular weight; most stable and inert |

#### Based on Decomposition Stage

| Type | Description |
|---|---|
| **Labile humus** | Easily decomposable; short-lived; quick nutrient release |
| **Stable humus** | Resistant to decomposition; long-lived; slow nutrient release |
</md></sn><sn><md>### Importance of Humus:

- **Nutrient retention:** High CEC holds Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, K⁺, NH₄⁺
- **Water retention:** Improves moisture holding capacity
- **Soil structure:** Binds particles into stable aggregates
- **Buffering:** Resists pH change
- **Microbial habitat:** Supports beneficial microorganisms
- **Carbon sequestration:** Stores carbon, mitigating climate change

**Example:**
In **Mull humus** (deciduous forests), earthworms mix humus with mineral soil, creating fertile, well-structured soil. In **Mor humus** (coniferous forests), acidic conditions slow decomposition, forming a thick raw humus layer.

**Conclusion:**
Humus is the **stable, colloidal organic matter** formed by decomposition. It is classified into **Mor, Moder, and Mull** (based on origin) and **fulvic acid, humic acid, and humin** (based on solubility). Humus is essential for soil fertility, structure, and nutrient retention.</md></sn>